      "biography": "Sing-Sing were an English indie pop and dream pop duo formed in London in 1997 by vocalist Lisa O’Neill and guitarist-vocalist Emma Anderson, formerly of Lush. Their music combined Anderson’s melodic guitar work and O’Neill’s airy vocals with elements of 1960s girl-group pop, folk, and electronica, creating a warm, understated sound marked by shimmering textures and gentle psychedelia. The group developed from early demos made with drummer Justin Welch and producer Mark Van Hoen, later working with a rotating cast of musicians.\n\nSing-Sing released their debut album, The Joy of Sing-Sing, in 2001 through Poptones, following singles including “Feels Like Summer” and “I’ll Be.” After issuing the independently financed Madame EP in 2004, they returned with Sing-Sing and I in 2005, an album that further refined their intimate, atmospheric style. Though the duo disbanded in 2007, Sing-Sing’s small but influential catalogue remains notable for connecting the traditions of British alternative pop, shoegaze-adjacent dreaminess, and electronic folk.",
