    "perplexity": {
      "description": "The Joy of Sing-Sing is the 2001 debut by Sing-Sing, the duo of vocalist Lisa O’Neill and former Lush guitarist and vocalist Emma Anderson. Produced by Mark van Hoen, it blends indie pop, dream pop, folk-inflected melody, and electronic production into a sound that is airy yet carefully constructed. Synthesizers take precedence over conventional guitar rock, drawing on both 1960s girl-group pop and 1980s dance music, while Anderson’s understated guitar textures and O’Neill’s delicate, sometimes shadowed vocals give the album an unmistakable dreamlike character.\n\nSongs such as “Tegan,” “Feels Like Summer,” and “Everything” balance bright hooks with layered harmonies, programmed rhythms, and subtly melancholic atmosphere. Contemporary reception highlighted the record’s inventive studio sound, its vocal interplay, and its connection to Lush without treating it as a continuation of that band’s style. The 2026 remaster, issued by Aerial Records for the album’s 25th anniversary, features newly remastered audio by Kevin Vanbergen, new artwork by Chris Bigg, and editions supplemented with B-sides and rarities, renewing attention on a distinctive and underappreciated British electronic-pop album.",
